[ ] Project workspaces to the application graph in Core =v5-projection $The core code change of Decision 43. Replace the v4 projection in clearhead-core rdf/project.rs and rdf/mod.rs with the mapping the spec defines, emitting no v4 terms. Prove it against the spec's fixture and shapes at Core's spec-conformance boundary, as the JSON schema conformance already runs, and check reasoner consistency with the ontology. Expect the graph to grow about tenfold per action; measure load and query time on this workspace before and after. ADDED 2026-10-01: the spec now requires times as written (no offset unless the file wrote one; date-only as xsd:date). Core's domain stores DateTime<Local>, so today's projection stamps the machine's offset and differs between time zones; the projection, or the domain, must keep what was written. CORRECTION 2026-10-02: the graph grows about 2x, not tenfold. Measured on the spec fixture: v4 emits 109 triples, 23 of them ws: storage facts that Decision 44 removes (86 left); the V5 expected graph has 172. Still measure load and query time, but expect no step change. RETARGETED 2026-10-02 by Decision 45: Core emits the application vocabulary, not CCO; most of today's v4 projection survives with renamed terms and the fixture's expected-app.ttl as its oracle. Keep times as written.$ !1 ^2026-10-01T20:22 <01a0fb89-fdcd-722f-a0ab-5399b7a3c0cb #01a0faa2-7184-74d1-ae6c-1959f4af7fe3
[ ] Move the saved queries to the application vocabulary =v5-queries $Rewrite every saved query in clearhead-cli src/queries and the CLI's sparql modules against the new graph, plus this workspace's .clearhead/queries/for-human.sparql. Row shapes stay as they are, so nvim and other clients do not change; the existing query tests are the net, so freeze their expected rows before rewriting. The competency queries in ontology v5/queries are the working templates. ADDED 2026-10-01: the graph will carry no storage facts (spec convention 3, the human's call: no file paths, lines, roots or calendar ids, since another backend such as sqlite would have none). Today's rows include source_file and source_line, used by nvim to jump to source, and some index queries join through the ws:Workspace node. Keep the rows unchanged by having the implementation add locations after the query, joining each row's IRI against the loaded workspace; remove every ws: pattern from the queries. REVISED 2026-10-01 with the human: rows lose source_file, source_line and charter_root instead of the CLI adding them back; clients get locations from the new locate command. The row shapes therefore do change for those columns, and nvim moves with them (nvim-locate). RETARGETED 2026-10-02 by Decision 45: queries use the application vocabulary (the spike's view queries are the template, about a third of v4's length); file and line stay in the graph, so rows keep their location columns and nvim is unchanged.$ !2 ^2026-10-01T20:22 <01a0fb02-e644-7317-88ad-1e76ecfb2b0e #01a0faa2-71a3-70a1-91c5-a485dedd4de2
[ ] Retire the v4 vocabulary everywhere =retire-v4 $Remove the v4 namespace constants and fixtures from clearhead-core, the ontology's v4 directory, examples and queries, its pytest suite and pyproject deps, and V4_DESIGN.md; point specifications/ontology.md readers at the ontology's docs/domain.md. Decide then whether clearhead.us keeps serving the v4 vocabulary for anyone holding its IRIs (cool URIs do not change) or drops it with the site.$ !3 ^2026-10-01T20:22 <01a0faa2-71a3-70a1-91c5-a485dedd4de2 #01a0faa2-71c0-7365-9b59-8baa79bf9766
[ ] Extend the graph fixture to recurring plans and objective states =v5-fixture-gaps $The graph fixture in specifications/examples/conformance/graph covers every action field and state, charters, objectives, contexts and waits, but not: an .ics recurring plan with a materialized occurrence (when/recurrence, RRULE text, occurrence as part of the recurring action), and an objective state, whose vocabulary objectives.md does not define yet (Decision 5 says objectives have state, confirmed by an agent). Settle the objective state values with the objectives-into-Core work, then add both to the workspace, expected.ttl and the shapes, with an invalid graph for each new rule. Decision 45: the gaps apply to both expected-app.ttl and expected.ttl.$ !3 ^2026-10-01T22:27 #01a0fb14-c5e6-73d8-9c0d-f56f11ce00a6
[ ] Show the actions that serve an objective =objective-actions-view $Moved out of the objectives action, 2026-10-01: answering it is a query (an objective's plans, then everything they contain), which V5 makes a has-continuant-part walk from the plans that name the objective. Write it as a saved query once v5-queries has moved the queries to the new graph, then surface it in the CLI and nvim like the other named queries.$ !3 ^2026-10-01T22:35 <01a0faa2-71a3-70a1-91c5-a485dedd4de2 #01a0fb1c-4455-7218-bc00-d4d38247de78
[ ] Distinguish scheduled from due in the mapping =scheduled-vs-due $Found by the query-surface spike, 2026-10-02: a scheduled time (not before) and a due time (by) both project as a condition whose description carries a datetime on its bearer, so the graph cannot tell a start from a deadline; only the UUIDv5 role, which is a hash, differs. Neither the shapes nor the reasoner caught it, since both are valid and consistent. Find standard terms that say which bound a time is (for example a description of a temporal interval that the time starts or finishes, CCO interval starts / interval finishes, or OWL-Time), update ontology.md, the fixture, the shapes, and add an invalid graph or a query to the gate that must recover the difference. Also decide how a date and a datetime compare, since times are now as written.$ !1 ^2026-10-02T00:28 #01a0fb83-8b3a-700c-8776-4c5749b3e82d
[ ] Define the application vocabulary in the spec =app-vocabulary $Per Decision 45: a fresh-named ClearHead application vocabulary, owned by the specification beside the JSON schemas. Start from the spike's view and v4, but complete: every DSL, charter and objective field and state, plus file and line (optional for another backend), so the CCO graph can be generated from it. Rewrite specifications/ontology.md around it (the application graph first; CCO as its meaning, mapped in the ontology repo), add its SHACL shapes, and add expected-app.ttl to the graph fixture: the workspace's exact application graph. The name waits on the human.$ !1 ^2026-10-02T00:35 <01a0fb83-8b3a-700c-8776-4c5749b3e82d #01a0fb89-fdba-7302-9609-92dfed6469d5
[ ] Map the application vocabulary to CCO in the ontology =app-to-cco $Per Decision 45 the ontology becomes the mapping: one SPARQL CONSTRUCT from the application vocabulary to CCO v2.2 and IAO, in the ontology repo, defining what each application term means. Helper-node IRIs must be computable inside SPARQL (no UUIDv5 there), for example a hash of owner and role; update the spec's Helper node IRIs and expected.ttl to match. Gate: the mapping of the fixture's expected-app.ttl is isomorphic to expected.ttl, which passes the CCO shapes, HermiT and the ontology's verify rules; wire it into validate-pinned. Consider rewriting the ontology's examples in the application vocabulary and mapping them for reasoning.$ !1 ^2026-10-02T00:35 <01a0fb89-fdba-7302-9609-92dfed6469d5 #01a0fb89-fdcd-722f-a0ab-5399b7a3c0cb
[ ] Name the application vocabulary $FOR THE HUMAN. Decision 45 wants a fresh name for the vocabulary people query. Recommended: the ClearHead application vocabulary, namespace https://clearhead.us/vocab/app/v1# with prefix app:, absorbing the retired ws: terms (file and line now belong to it). It reads as what it is, an application layer, and the v1 leaves room for a v2. Alternatives: https://clearhead.us/vocab/clearhead/v1# with prefix clearhead: (plainer, but sounds like the meaning layer, which is CCO's job); or a product word of your choosing. app-vocabulary waits on this.$ !1 +human ^2026-10-02T00:35 #01a0fb8a-37d6-74f8-9b7f-2558dfe00000
